# (function () {}).call(t)  (function () {}).apply(t, list)  Reflect.apply(function () {}, t, list): the built-in calls that operand once, then and there, so the function runs as often as the call does.
# What the call hands on to another function proves nothing: Array.prototype.forEach.call(list, callback) runs the callback for each element. Nor does a bound function, call through call,
# a receiver that may be a built-in, a second place, or a loop
